re singletons in some other sense; openness of the components is local connectedness, a separate hypothesis\"\nDEFECT: Singleton connected components are exactly the library’s definition of total disconnectedness. Moreover, openness of the components of the whole space is strictly weaker than local connectedness, not equivalent to it.\nEVIDENCE: The on-disk definition says that a space is totally disconnected when every connected component is a singleton. The later characterization states that local connectedness requires every component of every open subspace to be open, and its Remark says verbatim: “Openness of the components of $X$ alone is strictly weaker.” Explicitly, the closed topologist’s sine curve is compact, Hausdorff, connected and not locally connected; its sole component is the whole space and is therefore open.\nSEVERITY: fatal\nMINIMAL REPAIR: \"Nothing above says the components are open. If all components are singletons, then X is totally disconnected by definition. Local connectedness is a separate sufficient hypothesis that makes components open, but open components alone do not imply local connectedness.\"\n\n## Coverage\nItems read in full: lem-the-order-topology-on-an-ordinal, thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness, thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact, thm-quasicomponents-equal-components-in-a-compact-hausdorff-space\nItems not read, and why: none\nFacts checked against their target on disk: 34\nFindings: 3 fatal, 0 nonfatal"
